2026-09-01T16:30Z claude created, child of 0550

2026-09-01T13:18Z MinhHaDuong note blocker 0552 closed — Blocked-by removed.
2026-09-01T13:57Z claude note tranche 3 landed on fork branch tranche-3-extract-shim at e8784df (MinhHaDuong/zoteus); three commits — the shim, the suite, run-to-drain. 1067 tests green, tsc clean, eslint clean.
2026-09-01T13:57Z claude note constants disclosed as arbitrations, not measurements: the ruling declined a measurement, so PACE_WINDOW 8, PACE_DEGRADED_RATIO 2, PACE_DEGRADED_FLOOR_MS 50 and PACE_STEP_MS 250 are chosen and documented as such in fetch-pacer.ts. Only PACE_CEILING_MS 8000 is derived — below the row-claim TTL of 30x the ~1 s quantum, since a pacing delay long enough to expire the claim would duplicate the fetch it was slowing down.
2026-09-01T13:57Z claude note scope: the pipe is not built. The worker runs in-process behind ExtractDispatcher, which is where the process boundary falls — what crosses it is already an assignment and a result. Windows have no consumer until the segmenter exists (tranche 4, blocked on 0556), and a pipe protocol written against no consumer would claim the design was built. Also deferred: re-enqueueing the rows staleExtracts() finds, which needs a conductor-side sweep rather than a query.
2026-09-01T14:03Z claude note correction to the constants entry above: it named four chosen constants and there are five. PACE_MIN_SAMPLES = 4 — below which there is no verdict, since a median of two is not a median — is chosen on the same footing as the other four. Appended rather than corrected in place, per append-only.
2026-09-01T14:35Z claude note review round 1 (PR 156): three blockers fixed on the fork at ff1d41a. (1) a cut stream was stored as a short document — the scanner now reports whether the content string closed and a read that stopped inside it raises. (2) the back-off condemned healthy machines: an all-time-minimum baseline pinned near zero by a burst of instant answers. Root cause was a vacuous ratio test, not the minimum — against a baseline near zero everything exceeds twice nothing. Fixed by clamping the baseline at the degradation floor, plus a bounded horizon and a low quantile, and by measuring time-to-response rather than time-to-last-byte, which was confounded by document size. (3) lowerWorkerPriority and the pacer report had no call site; the pacer report now rides the drain report and the OS floor is a seam the forking entry point passes, off by default because until the worker is its own process the process it would renice is the one answering queries. Also: releaseExpiredClaims had no caller, so a hard-killed worker stranded its rows; the conductor now sweeps in poll.
2026-09-01T17:00Z review round 2 (PR 156): both parked blockers fixed on the fork at da3be32. (1) the claim race — Ledger.markDone/markFailed wrote WHERE wid = ? with no ownership check, so a worker whose claim releaseExpiredClaims had swept could land its late write over the worker that actually finished the row, older read winning because it arrived second. Completion now takes the ClaimTicket claim grants and guards on the triple holder + claimed_input + claim_expires_at, returning whether the write applied. The expiry is what carries the identity: one conductor spawns one worker under one holder string, so the re-claim after an expiry usually has the same name and input, and a claimed_by check alone passes the case that matters. ExtractStage keeps the tickets conductor-side, so the worker still holds nothing about writes; a rejected completion writes nothing at all and is counted on the drain report as staleCompletions. (2) the sibling truncation case — a stream cut after the content string closed but before the trailing page counts left truncated reading false on a document whose coverage is unknown; the scanner now requires the response object itself to close, counted over head plus tail with string literals skipped.
2026-09-01T17:00Z the claim TTL is now the ratified one, closing round 2's third finding. ExtractStage defaulted claimTtlMs to LEASE_TTL_MS — 20 s, the conductor election's lease constant reused for an unrelated purpose — where SPEC 5.2.5 pins the row claim at 30x the ~1 s quantum. CLAIM_TTL_MS in extract-stage.ts is that number and the default now reads from it; the code moved to the document, not the other way round, so no design number changed. fetch-pacer.ts's ceiling derivation gets back the 22 s margin it was reasoning about (8 s ceiling under a 30 s claim), where round 1 had corrected it to 12 s against the wrong constant.
2026-09-01T17:00Z positive controls for round 2, each run: stripping the ownership guard from the completion SQL turns six tests red — three in conductor-ledger.test.ts (swept-and-retaken, same-holder re-claim, a completed row completed twice) and three in conductor-extract.test.ts (the swept row finished by another stage, the swept row nobody retook, the three completion shapes) — while the control arms stay green, among them a live holder completing past its unswept TTL, which is the arm a guard that merely checked expiry would fail. Disabling the envelope-closed check turns the new stream arm red on its own. Gate on the fork at da3be32: 1 087 tests pass, 7 skipped, tsc clean, eslint clean.
2026-09-01T17:01Z Claude closed — round 2's two blockers fixed and mutation-verified on fork tranche-3-extract-shim at da3be32; claim TTL reconciled to SPEC 5.2.5's ratified 30x quantum
--- body ---
## Context

Third tranche (tracker 0550): the first pipeline worker. Sequential
extract, run-to-drain, FIFO within class — no lanes, no bands yet. The
§5.2.3 class order (metadata > notes/annotations > body) is an ORDER BY
and arrives here for free.

## Relevant files

- SPEC.md §5.2.4 (shim split: bookkeeping conductor-side, the
  whole-document GET worker-side; back-off ratified 2026-09-01),
  §5.2.3 (classes, first-with-text), DECISIONS.md 2026-09-01 (back-off
  ruling).

## Actions

1. Extract worker: spawned on queue work, drains, exits; one document
   GET at a time, nice 19 + background I/O class where the platform
   exposes one (per the ruling's capability wording).
2. Class order in the dispatch query; first-with-text choice function
   with stored skip reasons.
3. Latency back-off: rising local-API latency median inserts a decaying
   delay between fetches; reported on the instrument panel. No Web-API
   fallback (ticket 0505 is separate).
4. Activity-file yield between documents.

## Test

- Red first: against the replay fixture with programmed latency ramp,
  the inter-fetch delay appears and decays; without the ramp, none.

## Verification

- [x] Per item, nothing has body text extracted before its record
      bookkeeping exists (class order, per SPEC's per-item assertion).
      `Ledger.nextExtractOrder`'s NOT EXISTS clause; asserted over a
      whole interleaved run in `conductor-extract.test.ts`, where
      records are completed one at a time while the worker drains.
      Deleting the clause turns that assertion into 22 violations,
      which is how we know it is looking rather than passing vacuously.
- [x] Worker exits on stdin EOF and on lease-holder mismatch. Both
      through tranche 2's `WorkerOrphanGuard`, not re-implemented; with
      the control arm (parented and leased, it drains all five) and the
      lease case asserted with the pipe still open, which is the half
      the pipe cannot see.
- [x] A completion writes only to a row its author still holds (round 2).
      `markDone`/`markFailed` guard on the claim ticket and report
      whether the write applied; a rejected completion writes nothing,
      not even the extract row, and is counted on the drain report.
      Reproduced first as the failure, then as the fix, at both levels:
      the ledger primitive and a slow worker driven through `drain()`.
- [x] A stream that stopped anywhere before the response closed raises
      rather than storing a document (round 2). Round 1 covered the cut
      inside the text; the envelope check covers the cut after it, where
      `truncated` would otherwise read `false` on unknown coverage.

## Invariants

- Politeness constraints (web transport) untouched; upstream tests green.
  Held: the only `src/api/` change is one added method on the LOCAL
  client, `fetchFullTextStream`, and it goes through the same
  `RateLimitedFetcher` and its four-slot semaphore as every other read.
  Nothing under the web transport was touched. At round 2's tip
  (`da3be32`) 1 087 tests pass and 7 skip, `tsc` and `eslint` clean.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Extract stage merged on a fork branch, back-off test green.
      `tranche-3-extract-shim` at `da3be32` on MinhHaDuong/zoteus,
      branched from `tranche-2-conductor-election` as the tranche
      branches accumulate. Read "merged on a fork branch" as tranches 1
      and 2 were: the tranche branch is where the work lands, and no
      tranche branch has been merged to the fork's own main.
      `conductor-extract-backoff.test.ts` is green, and its floor arm
      was re-cut after the first draft proved undiscriminating — a flat
      slow library never trips the ratio either, so that arm would have
      passed with the floor deleted.
